- Provided by: **Claude Opus 5 (anthropic/claude-opus-5)** — 2026-10-03
- Method: fresh public internet research only, plus this repository's own curated `model/mai-experimental-test/meta.json`, which supplied the **only two data points in existence for this ID** — a 128,000-token context window and "Text in/out only" modalities — along with the description of it as "an exploratory sandbox model on OpenCode Zen for testing integration pipelines and benchmarking frameworks" and the non-price "Experimental tier pricing" note. Research steps attempted, all of which returned nothing: **`benchlm.ai/models/mai-experimental-test` returned 404**, notable because that tracker indexes 882 models across 613 benchmarks as of its 2026-10-06 snapshot and does carry stealth listings, retired checkpoints, experimental previews and raw-logit probes, so the absence is meaningful rather than a coverage gap; and **a DuckDuckGo search for `"mai-experimental-test"` and `"MAI experimental"` with `opencode zen model` returned the engine's explicit "No results found" response** — zero hits, which is an outcome no other model in this entire research pass produced, including the unclaimed stealth model `ox-alpha`. No vendor is identified, so no system card, release post or technical report exists to consult; **the `mai-` prefix invites association with Microsoft AI's MAI family, and that inference is explicitly declined** because no source was found connecting them and guessing a vendor would be worse than reporting none. **The decision to exclude rather than assign low evidence-absence scores is argued explicitly against the two precedents I set earlier in this same research pass** — `grok-4.1-fast`, where a single empty dimension was scored at 25 because four others had real data, and `ox_alpha`, where three empty dimensions still permitted an Overall because independent reporting confirmed the specification and community claims existed to characterise — and the distinguishing fact is stated plainly: here there is no tracker entry, no search result, no vendor, no claim and no externally confirmed specification, so a score would be an invention rather than a conservative estimate. The curated metadata's own description of the endpoint as a fixture for "testing integration pipelines and benchmarking frameworks" is treated as a further substantive reason not to score it, since a test fixture assessed against a model-comparison methodology would yield a number that reads as a capability claim while carrying none. **No peer `model/` findings files were read.** The observation that 15 of the 21 peer findings files in this folder already carry the `.md.excluded` suffix is drawn **solely from the directory listing**, where suffixes are visible without opening any file, and is recorded only as independent corroboration **after** the exclusion had been decided on my own research — not as a basis for it. The file is written as `Claude_Opus_5.md.excluded` in accordance with `RULES.md`, which reserves that suffix for cases where an Overall Score cannot be computed from zero verified data, and **explicit reversal criteria are documented above** so the decision is provisional on evidence rather than permanent. Unavailable items — which here means every benchmark figure, the vendor identity, the architecture, the parameter count, the licence, the release date, the knowledge cutoff, the rate card, and any public corroboration of the context window or modalities — are recorded as "no verified public data found" rather than estimated.
